				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${{{{{{\rm{AIC}}}}}}}_{{{{{{\rm{reg}}}}}}}=N{{{{{\rm{ln}}}}}}({MSE})+2k$$\end{document}AICreg=Nln(MSE)+2k, where N is the number of samples, and MSE is the mean squared error. The classification AIC was based on the likelihood of Bernoulli distribution, and was generalized to multi-class classification: \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${{{{{{\rm{AIC}}}}}}}_{{{{{{\rm{clf}}}}}}}=2\cdot {{{{{\rm{ln}}}}}}2\cdot N\cdot {H}_{p}({q}_{\theta })+2k$$\end{document}AICclf=2⋅ln2⋅N⋅Hp(qθ)+2k, where N is the number of samples, Hp is denotes cross entropy, and qθ is the estimated probability with parameters θ. The variable subset with the lowest AIC value was selected for each machine learning data set.
